[Music]  hello everyone and welcome to today's  webinar titled applications of multiplex  digital pcr  monitoring wastewater for stars kobe 2  and measuring bacterial gene expression  i'm christy joula flappard and i'll be  your moderator for today's event  today's educational web seminar is  presented by labyrinths and sponsored by  thermo fisher scientific  for more information on our sponsor  please visit thermofisher.com  now let's get started  you may participate by submitting as  many questions as you like at any time  you like during the presentation to do  so simply type them into the ask a  question box and click submit  i now present today's speaker  joan slanzewski professor of biology at  kenyon college for a complete biography  honor speaker please visit the biography  tab at the top of your screen  joan you may now begin your presentation  so thank you for that introduction and  i'm excited to be here today  describing our  experiences with developing wastewater  virus monitoring for a small community  so uh back in 2020 as the pandemic got  started  our small village of gambier in ohio was  interested in how we could  use  some of the signals available from  molecular biology to give information to  the community about the pandemic and you  can see here the  well what it looks like in gambiar ohio  people had a lot of questions like  should we wear masks the the mayor  had a big meeting as to whether there  should be a mask mandate for the village  and people wanted to know should they go  to a restaurant and  the information was always very delayed  so there's a dashboard of public cases  but it could be a a week or two before  you would see  the actual results of cases  so wastewater  monitoring for virus was something that  was being developed by a number of labs  in ohio  state of ohio is actually at the  forefront of this effort and so we  participated in that  so what we did is  we  developed a method to monitor  shed virus  in particular the rna from shed rna  viruses  from the village of gambier wastewater  treatment plant and actually the idea of  monitoring  viruses of  of  viral rna has been around for 20 years  and there were literature techniques to  do it  and  this particular virus there there were  public published  probes and and pcr  amplification probes that the same ones  that are used in clinical testing turned  out to be usable in wastewater  so  the village of gambier the wastewater  treatment plant  is actually it's a small plant only a  hundred thousand gallons per day and  it's a pretty tight plant there's not  too much infiltration so  it was a good case in a number of ways  now the village population  is small it happens to include about  two-thirds of the population are  undergraduate students from kenyon  college who live in residence halls in  very close quarters and so there's the  opportunity for rapid spreading of virus  there  now  during the past year  there was a a community dashboard that  posted  the results for active cases  both from gambier and then there was  also a college dashboard so we had case  data and the case data are shown in red  the published cases of course we know  that there are many cases of  of sars pope to that  that escape actual appearance  with  with symptoms  so  it was of interest to to monitor  can we detect the rna from virus of  people that are not infected as well as  those who are infected  so what you see here is  the results that we were able to obtain  with the thermo fisher instrument  you can see that the the blue curve  indicates  the log2 rna copies per liter that we  were able to measure  and we can see the  the progress of that over the last fall  and the corresponding data from  case reports in red  so the interesting thing is that as has  been shown elsewhere the ohio state  monitoring group and others have shown  that wastewater does act as a leading  indicator of cases so that  that  the viral rna appears in the wastewater  about a week ahead of typically case  reports  then once you have a spike in cases  the  wastewater rna levels do tend to lag  behind so it's considered a lagging  indicator of haste decline  what happens is that people that are  still shedding virus it  are  combined with people that  are new infections  so if you see if you see the  wastewater levels declining about a half  per week that's a good indication that  virus in general is declining that you  don't have new cases but the most  important thing for people in the  village was that you have this leading  indicator that you can see that there's  an infection spike starting before the  cases are actually reported and that's  useful for somebody considering well am  i going to go out to a restaurant this  weekend things like that  so what was the technology that we used  and so  basically we  went and obtained wastewater samples  from the influence of the village plant  and so we see here a wastewater sample  and a couple of tubes that have salt the  salt rapidly inactivates most pathogens  that would be in the waste water i  should add that we assume and this all  the literature has borne this out that  that the cyroscop2 virus  is rapidly inactivated in the wastewater  prior that what you're detecting is rna  from inactivated virus  there is not believed to be infectious  virus in the wastewater  we would obtain the samples  in the morning from an auto sampler that  had  cycled every 15 minutes  to get to get an average  sampling over the past 24 hours  and  we then took it back to the college  where  my students and i conducted  an rna purification  and the  purification method that we use there  are a variety of different tools for  purification but we we did the simplest  possible purification that we could find  which was from  from  the berkeley university  out there on on the internet  and so  basically what you do is you first  inactivate with nacl  then  you assume that viruses and other  pathogens are lysed and that any any rna  is removed from particles you filter  that wastewater now the filtration that  we used did retain  some small particles particles smaller  than five micrometers  so there is in the literature there's  some  sense that  some of the rna  viral rna is actually adherent to the  particulate matter of wastewater which  is a very complicated fluid so we're  capturing some of that  in our prep  then this is ethanol treated  and and  treated with a buffer that should remove  most of the rna from any  particulates and pass through  a lysate  through through the zymo silica spin  column this is a spin column  of silica that specifically attaches  nucleic acids and most other stuff pours  through  and in the  early way we took  concentrated rna the rna would have been  concentrated about 400 fold from the  original sample  and this was a process that was simple  enough that i could do and also  my undergraduate students we two  undergraduate students were actually  hired to help out doing this  so here is one of them bailey fitzgerald  and here she is  setting up to run the absolute q  instrument to  measure the amount of viral rna  in the samples  so the assay that we used was  assay that was developed by  carbonari before they were bought out by  thermo fisher  so the combination fourplex assay  includes  four different floors in four different  tacman probes for different primer sets  so  the primer sets n1 and n2 those are  based on the standard cdc primers that  are used in all the the swab tests  the bovine coronavirus was for a control  coronavirus there was some debate as to  the usefulness of that we eventually  decided that we did not  require that control that we felt it was  not really relevant to the stars co2  virus but then a very important control  is the  pepper  model virus  the which is a fecal model  indicator and so  that virus  was detected with uh the tie for so we  used those three and later versions of  the probemix were threeplex and included  just those three  floors  so in order to  to perform this  we did use the absolute q instrument in  which there are 16 wells  and  each well for a sample so for each  sample that the final sample volume is  to be nine microliters exactly so this  included the  the forex or three  the four excuse me the the  four-fold concentrated uh master mix  that includes the reverse transcriptase  then uh  20-fold concentrate of the probe mix  and the rna sample and there's room for  about two-thirds of the volume consists  of the rna sample  we also  ended up by second semester we started  using  a specific probe for a variant which was  the omicron variant so we did not do  that first semester in the data i showed  but for this current semester we've been  using the omicron twoplex probe which  was also developed by fissure and this  is based on the spike gene  a particular mutation the spike gene in  which glycine is replaced by  by  a different  amino acid and by aspartate and  so there are two probes that  compare the omicron variant result with  the standard reference which would have  been the delta variant at that time and  it turned out that both probes were able  to work under the same settings  so  i should also mention the rna sample  that we used we found  by experiment that the rna actually  required some dilution because  the rna has  had some pcr  pcr inhibitors in it and this has been  generally found with wastewater  monitoring so we experimented with  dilutions and we found that that if you  diluted five-fold or ten-fold that your  signal actually increased  so we ended up routinely diluting the  sample  in tea a buffer before including it  so here's what the instrument readout  looks like so for a typical run this  would take  two columns of the 16 well plate  and we would  have two independent samples of the  wastewater from that day  and  for each sample in column one  we would  assay  the fivefold dilution and the tenfold  dilution of the wastewater and then in  column two we did the same thing  but  used the omicron probe test instead the  cycle parameters you see are pretty  common parameters and we found that the  the 55 degree  annealing step is actually  pretty important because the the lower  the temperature actually it improves  some of the separation of the signal  cloud of of reactions from the  background  and sometimes using a lower temperature  than that of the  recommendation for the tacman probes was  a good thing and we found that 55  degrees worked well for both of our  probes  so here's a close look at what the data  look like for individual samples  so  you can see for example for the n1 probe  that  that the the black square represents the  20 000 plus  reaction wells and each dot it  represents one reaction where there was  a pcr amplification  and we assume that each of those  reactions represents  one molecule of viru of viral rna from  the original sample so that's a pretty  powerful techn technique where you can  be counting individual rna molecules out  of your sample  and in the  the upper diagrams you can see the  poisson distribution represented so  the positive signal the positive  reaction  wells are indicated  above and then below the threshold you  see the large bulk  of the 20 000 wells  did not react and you see a similar  picture for the n2 probe  the  the hex 4 and then the ppmov is is the  control saying that yes we have  have  significant fecal wastewater content  here and you could tell that worked  because when students went away for  vacation students were actually  about two-thirds of the population so  the ppmov fecal probe went way way down  when the students were out of town  so here we see  a an overall picture of the results  from  [Music]  a couple of days that we looked at we  also compared  a  recent day's results in january january  12th with an with a frozen sample from  september  so if we look at the first at the left  half of the slide we see  for  for  january  we see the results of  the  threeplex probe and we can see that for  both  the  the hex and the fam okay the  the blue and the green you can see that  there is definite signal there so there  was quite a bit of  wastewater virus on those dates and that  was when students had just came back and  we had had a spike of cases shortly  thereafter  and so we can also see the control as  well now on the right hand side we see  the results of the omicron probe set  compared with  the older september date and  so  so the blue floor is the one that  detects the omicron specific probe and  you can see that  that on january 12 that probe shows  specific reactions where whereas the  control from the from the older virus  variant shows nothing  and  by contrast the frozen september sample  shows  positive results for the delta variant  and nothing for the omicron variant so  this shows that in fact we were very  precisely able to see  and there was another there was another  day also where it was mixed okay where  it looked like there was some delta left  and some omicron coming in so you really  can see  novel variants just by shifting your  probe  in terms of controls  there there we use two kinds of controls  one is the fecal control so that for  instance in in this well you can see a  little bit of  a couple of  special counts that shouldn't be there  so i did talk with the students  afterward about  making sure that the loading was good  and how to keep pipettes uncontaminated  um  the other kind of control is is your  waste water good so the the fact that  you we all we always found actually  increasing counts for the tenfold  dilution versus the five-fold dilution  means yes that's looking like real  wastewater because you're seeing the  result of  inhibitors as well  so we relied on  on internal self-control as well as the  the presence of the fecal control in the  threeplex probe  so for overall summary of what we would  have seen on that on on those days  so  we had essentially  four  replicas  technical replicas looking at  the n1 and n2 probe sets it was pretty  consistent that the n2 probe set showed  higher numbers and we generally use that  one for our community reporting you can  also see it was clear that that we were  seeing  omicron on the recent date and delta on  the older days  so  at this point i would say that  that we are continuing to use  the wastewater data that it continues to  be considered to be of use by the  community in fact  what we did is we we posted these data  on the  timeline graphs that you saw earlier and  uh people are saying well it's good to  have these data especially as the  community opens up and not everybody's  wearing a mask anymore and they don't  report all the cases community members  consider this really helpful to have and  have been able to understand  at the level that is presented we also  have an email list where we present  explanations of it  in addition  it's really important to know that this  is it's fairly straightforward and easy  to do this kind of monitoring i have two  undergrads who who conduct the analysis  and has also it's drawn interest in  further kinds of research  using this technology so what we're  doing now at kenyon is we're applying to  other questions  for example  drug resistance  and how do we deal with drug resistance  and the effectiveness  of  of antibiotics and multi-drug resistance  genes in fact  some reports are coming out showing that  actually  a lot a lot there will be a lot more  uh fatalities and morbidity due to  drug resistant bacteria in the next few  years than to something like covid this  is  really a pressing problem in fact it's  been amplified in the hospitals because  as more people go to the hospitals with  covid  then the the hospitals are are not  following up on drug resistance as well  as they should so we're looking at  research questions related to drug  resistant bacteria and a particular  question we're studying in our lab is  how do oral pharmaceuticals  and as well as phytochemicals in our  foods affect  the bacterial expression of drug  resistance genes  so when you ingest food you have all  kinds of phytochemicals including  organic acids and we have a hypothesis  that these organic acids affect  the  relative fitness of  the multi-drug resistance phenotypes in  bacteria and therefore can alter the gut  microbiome and make you more or less  drug resistant in your microbiome  so  we're interested in looking at  multi-drug efflux pumps which are driven  primarily by the proton potential and  the proton potential is affected by  organic acids that leak hydrogen ions  into the cell  so we're looking at  the specific question of how expression  of these drug pumps is affected  by  various compounds and so  this is an approach where we can use the  same  the same configured assay this four flex  acid using four different floors and  four different teclan probes  we can use  this to  simultaneously assay three different  multi-drug pump expressions as well as  the rpob control that's the rna  polymerase enzyme  expression as a control and so we're  just developing this application now but  we find that the data actually looked  very similar to the wastewater data  we designed specific  probes in this case we had techman  probes designed by a company that's  expert in that and  it  i must say that even on our first try we  got four tacman probes that work  well and they work actually following  the same assay configuration that was  used for the wastewater  samples  so we can use this as a to screen rna  preps from  bacteria cultured under  various conditions of ph and drug  exposure and then we can we can screen  many different conditions with this  four-plex assay and then identify the  most promising conditions for later  rna-seq follow-up  so we're finding the absolute q  and the four flex assays to be a really  exciting application in basic research  as well as for community wastewater  monitoring  thank you and i look forward to  to  questions in the question and answer  period  i would like to acknowledge all those  excuse me  so i would like to acknowledge all the  support that we've received from the  ohio coronavirus wastewater monitoring  network  we received a sub award from the ohio  water resources center and the ohio  department of health  and  the village of gambier was very  supportive and encouraging we also  received funds from kenyan college  office of the provost and i would  especially like to thank my students  bailey fitzgerald and lang lang yang lu  lu for their work in this wastewater  monitoring  thank you joan for that informative  presentation we will now start the live  q a portion of our webinar if you have a  question you'd like to ask please do so  now just click on that ask a question  box and click submit we will answer as  many of your questions as we have time  for  so joe we have some great questions  coming in i'm going to start with this  one  why did you select digital pcr as a  technology to quantify nucleic acids for  these applications  well digital pcr was recommended  by  some of the ohio state professors with  the  wastewater monitoring network  and  i had already previously done wastewater  monitoring by sending the samples out to  the lumen ultra company but  that that took a week's turnaround and  so i was impressed by the fact that this  technique the digital pcr  could count individual molecules of rna  and that it required a small number of  controls  and  so it looked like something that  would be easily doable something that  would be something that could be learned  to do easily and could give rapid  results  thank you our next question what were  the impacts  with the data collected for this study  on the community and  do you see this method being used in the  future as we hopefully transition into  an endemic phase of this pandemic  well the most important impact of the  data collection was  the confidence of the village community  and  the kenyan community of many kenyan  employees as well as students and the  village  saw this as  as  providing a picture of  the pandemic for them and gave them more  confidence in the overall kinds of data  that they were getting and it it helped  enforce the village mass mandate to say  well even if there isn't a case reported  today the wastewater shows there's still  virus present in the community things  like that  so in the future there's a lot of  interest in wastewater monitoring for a  whole range of viruses it can be used to  monitor influenza  viruses and  and enteric viruses noroviruses as well  so i think that in the future  that there will be a lot more of this  kind of technology  and joan what other environmental  testing applications do you see digital  pcr being useful for  so digital pcr is useful for all kinds  of environmental science for for  measuring small quantities of rna and  dna molecules  so  i think that it can also be used to  measure viruses in natural water bodies  we do  do  microbiome analysis of streams and pawns  for example  thank you  and what are the advantages of using  multiplex digital pcr over other methods  to measure gene expression  well the biggest advantage is that it  can be done rapidly  on  many samples and you get you get data  data back  soon and  it's it it has the the ability to  measure single molecules so the amounts  of rna that you use are tiny  we found that the amounts of rna that we  need for our for our bacterial preps for  example it might be  one thousandth of an rna prep is what  you need  for a run  and so the fact that you need such small  amounts of rna is really great for  research applications there are now rna  micro preps that have been devised  that  can  obtain  very small amounts of rna from a large  range of samples and you couldn't have  afforded to do this before so the fact  that you can get many different samples  from different conditions is really good  and  the fact that you can get this  information and then it also serves as a  cross-check for the rna-seq applications  because it's a different technique  that provides information in a different  way compared to the rna-seq  approach to gene expression  joan you've given us so much information  today we do have time for one final  question and we'd love to know what  additional information do you gain from  using this technology  so the main additional information  that we gain is that we can  we can  target specific  genes  in a system and ask questions about  those specific genes over a large number  of conditions say a range of  concentrations  of  an exposure pharmaceutical  so  it it's a technique that can enable us  to target specific genes and to detect  the smallest quantity of expression of  them  and of course you could also use the  technique to query dna  samples as well so that i have  colleagues that are doing  a mapping of dna  long  long distance mapping of dna in in  chromosomes and you can use the  technique for that as well  thank you joan now do you have any final  comments for our audience today  well i would just like to say that if if  you know any young people considering  colleges who want to do  science research that kenyan college is  a great place  to send them that they will get a real  science education and get  participation in state of the art  research and get to use great  instruments like the absolute cue in  their research  thank you again to our speaker joan  flandusky for her time today and for her  important research  we'd also like to thank lab roots and  our sponsor thermo fisher for  underwriting today's educational webcast  now before we go i do want to thank our  audience for joining us today and for  their interesting questions  questions we did not have time for today  and those submitted during the on-demand  period they will be addressed by the  speaker via the contact information you  provided at the time of registration  this webcast can be viewed on demand and  lab routes will alert you via email when  it's available for replay we encourage  you to share that email with your  colleagues who may have missed today's  live event thank you again for joining  us have a great day  you

